L('malta', 'Malta', 'Italy/Rome', (14.51, 35.9),
  P('Temple builders', -5900, -700, short='Temples',
    note='Farmers from Sicily reached the islands about 5900 BC. Between about 3600 and 2500 BC their descendants raised great stone temples such as Ġgantija, older than the pyramids of Egypt, and carved the underground Hypogeum; Bronze Age newcomers followed.'),
  P('Phoenicians and Carthage', -700, -218, '~Carthaginian Empire', '~Carthage', short='Phoenicians',
    note='Phoenician traders settled the islands around 700 BC and worshipped Astarte at the old temple site of Tas-Silġ; from the 500s BC Malta was ruled by Carthage.'),
  P('Roman Malta', -218, 535, '~Roman Republic', '~Rome', '~Roman Empire', '~Rome (Maximian)', '~Western Roman Empire', '~Vandals',
    short='Roman',
    note='Rome took Malta from Carthage in 218 BC. Tradition says the apostle Paul was shipwrecked here around AD 60 and converted the islanders; the catacombs of Rabat date from the late empire, before Vandals and Goths took the islands.'),
  P('Byzantine Malta', 535, 870, '~Eastern Roman Empire', '~Byzantine Empire', short='Byzantine',
    note='Belisarius won Malta for the Byzantine Empire in 535, and for three centuries it was a naval outpost and a place of exile.'),
  P('Arab Malta', 870, 1091, '~Aghlabid Emirate', '~Emirate of Sicily', short='Arab',
    note='The Aghlabids of Tunisia took Malta in 870 and left it almost empty, until Muslims from Sicily resettled it about 1048. They brought new crops and irrigation, and their Arabic grew into the Maltese language.'),
  P('Normans and the Kingdom of Sicily', 1091, 1530, '~Sicily', short='Kingdom of Sicily',
    note='Count Roger the Norman landed in 1091, and in 1127 his son made Malta part of Sicily. It passed with Sicily to the Swabians, the Angevins and the kings of Aragon, while the local nobles ruled from Mdina.'),
  P('Knights of St John', 1530, 1798, short='Knights',
    note='Charles V gave Malta to the Knights Hospitaller in 1530. They beat off the Ottoman Great Siege of 1565, then built Valletta and the Co-Cathedral of St John, and ruled as crusading monks until Napoleon took the island in 1798.'),
  P('British Malta', 1798, 1964, 'Malta', short='British',
    note='The Maltese rose against the French, and Britain, called in to help, kept the islands as the base of its Mediterranean fleet. Malta held out through two years of bombing and siege in the Second World War, and the whole island was awarded the George Cross in 1942.'),
  P('Independent Malta', 1964, None, 'Malta', short='Independent',
    note='Malta became independent in 1964 and a republic in 1974; the last British forces left in 1979. It joined the European Union in 2004 and adopted the euro in 2008.'),
)
